"""Tasawwuf and Tazkiyah: broadening the Tazkiyah course (27 Nov 2026). Broad, not one tariqah: what tasawwuf is,
its roots, the stations, sincerity, the great tariqahs, the shaykh, states and karamat, and an invitation to seek a path."""
RQ = "al-Risalah al-Qushayriyyah"

LESSONS = {
 "tw_what": ("What is tasawwuf?", "Ihsan, the third level of the religion, and the purification of the heart.", [
   ("Ihsan", "When Jibril عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ asked about the religion, the Prophet ﷺ taught Islam (the outward acts), iman (belief), and ihsan: 'that you worship Allah as though you see Him, and though you do not see Him, He sees you.' Tasawwuf is the science of ihsan.", "Sahih Muslim, Book of Faith"),
   ("Its subject", "Fiqh governs the limbs, 'aqidah the beliefs, and tasawwuf the heart: purifying it of pride, envy, showing off and love of the world, and adorning it with sincerity, humility, patience, gratitude and love of Allah.", f"{RQ}, introduction"),
   ("A definition", "Al-Junayd رَحِمَهُ اللَّهُ was asked about tasawwuf and said: that the Truth makes you die to yourself and live by Him. Others said: it is good character, so whoever surpasses you in character surpasses you in tasawwuf.", f"{RQ}, chapter on tasawwuf"),
   ("Bound by the Sunnah", "Al-Junayd said: this knowledge of ours is bound by the Book and the Sunnah. Whoever has not memorised the Qur'an and written hadith is not to be followed in it.", f"{RQ}, the masters of the path"),
  ], [
   ("In the hadith of Jibril, ihsan is…", "To worship Allah as though you see Him", ["To give charity often", "To fast every Monday", "To memorise the Qur'an"], "And though you do not see Him, He sees you.", "Sahih Muslim, Book of Faith"),
   ("Tasawwuf is mainly concerned with…", "Purifying the heart", ["Rulings of trade", "Arabic grammar", "Rules of inheritance"], "Fiqh for the limbs, 'aqidah for belief, tasawwuf for the heart.", f"{RQ}, introduction"),
   ("Al-Junayd said this knowledge is bound by…", "The Book and the Sunnah", ["Dreams", "Local customs", "The shaykh's opinion alone"], "No path is sound outside the Shari'ah.", f"{RQ}, the masters of the path"),
  ]),
 "tw_roots": ("Its roots", "Tasawwuf in the Qur'an, the Sunnah and the early generations.", [
   ("In the Qur'an", "Allah sent the Prophet ﷺ to recite His verses, purify the people (tazkiyah), and teach them the Book and wisdom. 'Successful is the one who purifies it.'", "Qur'an, al-Jumu'ah 62:2"),
   ("In the Prophet ﷺ", "He ﷺ withdrew to Hira' before revelation, stood in prayer until his feet swelled, and lived simply though the world was offered to him. He said: there is a piece of flesh in the body; if it is sound, the whole body is sound, and that is the heart.", "Sahih al-Bukhari, Book of Faith"),
   ("In the Companions", "The People of the Suffah lived in the Prophet's masjid ﷺ for worship and learning. Abu Bakr رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ gave all his wealth; Abu Dharr رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ was known for his detachment.", f"{RQ}, introduction"),
   ("The name", "The name 'Sufi' became common in the second century, when the world spread among people and those who devoted themselves to worship were singled out by it, just as the names 'fiqh' and 'tafsir' came later for sciences whose substance was there from the start.", f"{RQ}, introduction"),
   ("The early masters", "Al-Hasan al-Basri, Rabi'ah al-'Adawiyyah, al-Fudayl ibn 'Iyad, Ma'ruf al-Karkhi, al-Sari al-Saqati and al-Junayd رَحِمَهُمُ اللَّهُ are among the early people of the path. Al-Qushayri gathered their sayings in his Risalah, and al-Ghazali joined fiqh and tasawwuf in the Ihya'.", f"{RQ}, the masters of the path"),
  ], [
   ("Which three tasks of the Prophet ﷺ does al-Jumu'ah 2 mention?", "Reciting the verses, purifying people, teaching the Book and wisdom", ["Building, trading, travelling", "Fighting, ruling, judging", "Fasting, praying, giving zakat"], "Tazkiyah is one of the Prophet's missions ﷺ.", "Qur'an, al-Jumu'ah 62:2"),
   ("The name 'Sufi' being later than the Prophet ﷺ means…", "Nothing against it, like the names fiqh and tafsir", ["It is an innovation in belief", "It has no basis", "It was forbidden"], "The substance was there; the name came later.", f"{RQ}, introduction"),
   ("Which piece of flesh did the Prophet ﷺ say makes the body sound?", "The heart", ["The tongue", "The stomach", "The eye"], "Narrated by al-Bukhari and Muslim.", "Sahih al-Bukhari, Book of Faith"),
  ]),
 "tw_fear": ("Fear and hope", "The two wings of the believer.", [
   ("Two wings", "The believer flies to Allah with two wings: fear of His punishment, which keeps him from sin, and hope in His mercy, which keeps him from despair. Without fear he becomes careless; without hope he gives up.", f"{RQ}, chapter on fear"),
   ("Fear", "Fear is the heart's pain at expecting something disliked. Its fruit is leaving sins. Al-Qushayri: the one who fears is not the one who weeps and wipes his eyes, but the one who leaves what he fears he will be punished for.", f"{RQ}, chapter on fear"),
   ("Hope", "Hope is the heart's attachment to something loved that is coming, with effort to reach it. Hope without action is only wishful thinking.", f"{RQ}, chapter on hope"),
   ("Balance", "In health and youth, let fear be stronger; at death, let hope be stronger. The Prophet ﷺ said: let none of you die except thinking well of Allah.", "Sahih Muslim, Book of Paradise"),
  ], [
   ("What is the fruit of true fear of Allah?", "Leaving sins", ["Weeping only", "Despairing of mercy", "Avoiding people"], "Fear is measured by what it makes you leave.", f"{RQ}, chapter on fear"),
   ("Hope without action is…", "Only wishful thinking", ["True hope", "Trust in Allah", "Better than fear"], "Real hope strives for what it hopes for.", f"{RQ}, chapter on hope"),
   ("At the time of death, which should be stronger?", "Hope in Allah's mercy", ["Fear of punishment", "Neither", "Worry about wealth"], "'Let none of you die except thinking well of Allah.'", "Sahih Muslim, Book of Paradise"),
  ]),
 "tw_stations": ("The stations of the heart", "Scrupulousness, detachment, gratitude, contentment and love.", [
   ("Scrupulousness (wara')", "Leaving what is doubtful for fear of the haram. 'Leave what makes you doubt for what does not.'", f"{RQ}, chapter on scrupulousness"),
   ("Detachment (zuhd)", "Not that your hand is empty of the world, but that your heart is. Use the world, but do not let it own you.", f"{RQ}, chapter on detachment"),
   ("Gratitude (shukr)", "Gratitude is with the heart (knowing every blessing is from Allah), the tongue (praising Him) and the limbs (using His blessings in obedience). 'If you are grateful, I will surely increase you.'", f"{RQ}, chapter on gratitude"),
   ("Contentment (rida)", "Being content with what Allah decrees, without objecting in the heart. It does not mean not making du'a or not trying to change things; it means not resenting His choice.", f"{RQ}, chapter on contentment"),
   ("Love (mahabbah)", "'He loves them and they love Him.' The sign of loving Allah is following His Messenger ﷺ: 'If you love Allah, follow me, and Allah will love you.'", f"{RQ}, chapter on love"),
  ], [
   ("Zuhd means…", "The world is in your hand but not in your heart", ["Owning nothing at all", "Not working", "Avoiding people"], "Detachment is of the heart.", f"{RQ}, chapter on detachment"),
   ("Gratitude is shown with…", "The heart, the tongue and the limbs", ["Words only", "Wealth only", "The heart only"], "Using blessings in obedience is gratitude of the limbs.", f"{RQ}, chapter on gratitude"),
   ("What is the sign of loving Allah?", "Following the Messenger ﷺ", ["Feeling emotional in dhikr", "Seeing dreams", "Wearing special clothes"], "Al 'Imran 31.", "Qur'an, Al 'Imran 3:31"),
   ("Does contentment (rida) mean you stop making du'a?", "No; it means not resenting Allah's decree", ["Yes", "Only for illness", "Only for wealth"], "You still ask and strive.", f"{RQ}, chapter on contentment"),
  ]),
 "tw_ikhlas": ("Sincerity and watching the self", "Ikhlas, muraqabah and muhasabah.", [
   ("Sincerity (ikhlas)", "Doing the deed for Allah alone, not for people's praise. Al-Qushayri: sincerity is to single out Allah as the only aim in obedience. Deeds are by intentions.", f"{RQ}, chapter on sincerity"),
   ("Watchfulness (muraqabah)", "Knowing at every moment that Allah sees you. This is the root of ihsan: 'though you do not see Him, He sees you.' Whoever keeps it guards his limbs and his thoughts.", f"{RQ}, chapter on watchfulness"),
   ("Reckoning (muhasabah)", "'Umar رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ said: take account of yourselves before you are taken to account. Each night, look back: what did I do for Allah today, and what must I repent of?", "Sunan al-Tirmidhi, Book of the Description of the Resurrection"),
   ("Hidden showing off", "Showing off can be hidden: loving to be praised, being more careful in worship when seen, hinting at one's good deeds. Keep some good deeds secret between you and Allah.", "Ihya' 'Ulum al-Din, Book of Condemning Status and Showing Off"),
  ], [
   ("Sincerity means doing the deed…", "For Allah alone", ["Perfectly", "In public", "Often"], "Deeds are by intentions.", f"{RQ}, chapter on sincerity"),
   ("'Take account of yourselves before you are taken to account' was said by…", "'Umar ibn al-Khattab رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ", ["Abu Hanifah رَحِمَهُ اللَّهُ", "al-Ghazali رَحِمَهُ اللَّهُ", "Bilal رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ"], "Narrated by al-Tirmidhi.", "Sunan al-Tirmidhi, Book of the Description of the Resurrection"),
   ("Which is a sign of hidden showing off?", "Praying more carefully when people are watching", ["Praying on time", "Praying in congregation", "Keeping worship secret"], "Keep some deeds secret.", "Ihya' 'Ulum al-Din, Book of Condemning Status and Showing Off"),
  ]),
 "tw_tariqah": ("The tariqahs", "The great Sufi paths, and what they share.", [
   ("What a tariqah is", "A tariqah ('path') is a school of tazkiyah: a chain (silsilah) of teachers reaching back to the Prophet ﷺ, with a set of practices of dhikr, companionship and discipline, under a guide. Like the schools of fiqh, they differ in method, not in religion.", f"{RQ}, chapter on companionship"),
   ("The great tariqahs", "Among the best known: the Qadiri (Shaykh 'Abd al-Qadir al-Jilani, Baghdad, d. 561 AH), the Rifa'i (Ahmad al-Rifa'i, Iraq, d. 578 AH), the Suhrawardi (Shihab al-Din 'Umar al-Suhrawardi, Baghdad, d. 632 AH), the Chishti (spread in India by Khwajah Mu'in al-Din Chishti, d. 633 AH), the Shadhili (Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili, d. 656 AH) and the Naqshbandi (Baha' al-Din Naqshband, Bukhara, d. 791 AH). رَحِمَهُمُ اللَّهُ.", "Tarikh al-Khulafa', and the biographies of the masters"),
   ("Their chains", "Most tariqahs trace their chain through 'Ali رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ; the Naqshbandi chain runs through Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ. Every sound chain ends with the Prophet ﷺ.", f"{RQ}, the masters of the path"),
   ("Different methods", "Some emphasise dhikr aloud in gatherings, others silent dhikr of the heart; some emphasise service, others study or seclusion. The aim is one: a heart present with Allah and a life upon the Sunnah.", f"{RQ}, chapter on dhikr"),
   ("Respect for all", "The Ahl al-Sunnah respect every sound tariqah and its masters. Belonging to one path never means looking down on another, or on Muslims who follow none.", "Ihya' 'Ulum al-Din, Book of Companionship"),
  ], [
   ("What is a silsilah?", "A chain of teachers going back to the Prophet ﷺ", ["A type of dhikr bead", "A Sufi building", "A book of poetry"], "Every sound tariqah has a connected chain.", f"{RQ}, the masters of the path"),
   ("Through which Companion does the Naqshbandi chain pass?", "Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ", ["'Umar رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ", "Mu'awiyah رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ", "Khalid ibn al-Walid رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ"], "Most other chains pass through 'Ali رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ.", f"{RQ}, the masters of the path"),
   ("The Qadiri tariqah is named after…", "Shaykh 'Abd al-Qadir al-Jilani رَحِمَهُ اللَّهُ", ["Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili", "Mu'in al-Din Chishti", "Baha' al-Din Naqshband"], "He taught in Baghdad and died in 561 AH.", "Tarikh al-Khulafa', and the biographies of the masters"),
   ("The tariqahs differ in…", "Method, not in religion", ["Their Qur'an", "Their prophet", "Their prayer times"], "Like the schools of fiqh.", f"{RQ}, chapter on companionship"),
  ]),
 "tw_shaykh": ("The shaykh and companionship", "Why the masters advise taking a guide, and how to know a true one.", [
   ("Why a guide", "Faults of the self are hard to see in oneself. As one learns fiqh from a faqih and Qur'an from a qari, one learns the path from someone who has travelled it. Al-Ghazali, after searching alone, found his cure with the people of the path.", "al-Munqidh min al-Dalal, the way of the Sufis"),
   ("Companionship (suhbah)", "The Companions were made Companions by sitting with the Prophet ﷺ. Allah says: keep yourself patiently with those who call on their Lord morning and evening, and: be with the truthful.", "Qur'an, al-Kahf 18:28"),
   ("Signs of a true guide", "He holds firmly to the Shari'ah, has sound knowledge of the deen, a connected chain, good character, and his company increases you in remembrance of Allah and following the Sunnah. The Prophet ﷺ said the best of people are those who, when seen, remind you of Allah.", "Sunan Ibn Majah, Book of Zuhd"),
   ("Warning signs", "Anyone who neglects the prayer, permits the haram, takes people's wealth, demands blind obedience in sin, or says the Shari'ah is not for him is no guide, whatever wonders are claimed.", f"{RQ}, counsel to disciples"),
   ("The bay'ah", "Taking a pledge (bay'ah) with a shaykh is a promise to keep to the Shari'ah and the practices of the path under his guidance. The Companions pledged to the Prophet ﷺ on more than belief, such as 'not to ask people for anything'.", "Sahih Muslim, Book of Zakat"),
  ], [
   ("What is a sign of a true spiritual guide?", "His company increases you in remembrance of Allah and the Sunnah", ["He has many followers online", "He performs wonders", "He says the Shari'ah does not apply to him"], "The best are those whose sight reminds you of Allah.", "Sunan Ibn Majah, Book of Zuhd"),
   ("Someone claiming to be a shaykh asks you to commit a sin. You…", "Refuse; he is no guide", ["Obey, he knows better", "Obey once to test him", "Ask him to pray for you first"], "No one is obeyed in disobedience to Allah.", f"{RQ}, counsel to disciples"),
   ("Which verse tells us to keep company with those who call on their Lord?", "Al-Kahf 28", ["Al-Baqarah 255", "Al-Ikhlas 1", "Al-Nas 1"], "'Keep yourself patiently with those who call on their Lord morning and evening.'", "Qur'an, al-Kahf 18:28"),
  ]),
 "tw_states": ("States, wonders and the Shari'ah", "Keeping experiences in their place.", [
   ("States and stations", "A state (hal) is something that comes over the heart as a gift, like awe or longing, and passes; a station (maqam) is what is earned by effort and stays, like repentance and patience. Neither is a goal in itself.", f"{RQ}, chapter on states and stations"),
   ("Karamat are real", "Ahl al-Sunnah believe Allah honours His friends with wonders (karamat), as with Maryam's provision and the companions of the cave. A karamah of a wali is a sign of the truth of the Prophet ﷺ he follows.", f"{RQ}, chapter on karamat"),
   ("But they are not the measure", "Abu Yazid al-Bistami said: if you see a man given wonders, even sitting cross-legged in the air, do not be deceived until you see how he keeps the commands and prohibitions and the limits of the Shari'ah.", f"{RQ}, the masters of the path"),
   ("Dreams and unveilings", "A good dream can be glad tidings, but dreams and inner experiences never make anything halal or haram, and never override the Qur'an, the Sunnah or the scholars.", f"{RQ}, chapter on dreams"),
  ], [
   ("A man performs wonders but ignores the Shari'ah. He is…", "Not to be followed", ["A great wali", "Above the law", "Excused by his state"], "Abu Yazid: look at how he keeps the commands and prohibitions.", f"{RQ}, the masters of the path"),
   ("Can a dream make something halal or haram?", "No", ["Yes, if the dreamer is pious", "Yes, if repeated three times", "Only for the dreamer"], "Rulings come from the Qur'an and Sunnah.", f"{RQ}, chapter on dreams"),
   ("A station (maqam) differs from a state (hal) in that it is…", "Earned by effort and lasting", ["A passing gift", "Only for prophets", "A dream"], "States come and go; stations are earned.", f"{RQ}, chapter on states and stations"),
  ]),
 "tw_question": ("\"Isn't tasawwuf an innovation?\"", "Answering the common objection with knowledge and good manners.", [
   ("The objection", "Some say tasawwuf is not from Islam because the word is not in the Qur'an or hadith, or because of things some people calling themselves Sufis have done.", f"{RQ}, introduction"),
   ("The substance is the Sunnah", "Ihsan, tazkiyah, dhikr, repentance, sincerity and good character are in the Qur'an and Sunnah. A new name for a sound science is like 'fiqh', 'usul' or 'tajwid'.", "Sahih Muslim, Book of Faith"),
   ("Judge by the scholars", "The great scholars of Ahl al-Sunnah, such as al-Qushayri, al-Ghazali, al-Nawawi, Ibn 'Abidin and Shah Wali Allah, praised and practised tasawwuf bound by the Shari'ah, and criticised anything against it. Wrong actions by some are judged by the Shari'ah, not blamed on the science.", "al-Munqidh min al-Dalal, the way of the Sufis"),
   ("With good manners", "Answer with knowledge and gentleness, not argument for its own sake. The best invitation to tasawwuf is the character of the one who practises it.", "Ihya' 'Ulum al-Din, Book of Companionship"),
  ], [
   ("The word 'tasawwuf' coming later makes it…", "No more an innovation than the names 'fiqh' or 'tajwid'", ["Haram", "Disbelief", "Unknown to scholars"], "The substance is from the Qur'an and Sunnah.", "Sahih Muslim, Book of Faith"),
   ("Wrong actions by some who call themselves Sufis are…", "Judged by the Shari'ah, not blamed on the science", ["Proof tasawwuf is false", "Acceptable", "Ignored"], "The same is true of any science.", "al-Munqidh min al-Dalal, the way of the Sufis"),
   ("What is the best invitation to tasawwuf?", "The good character of those who practise it", ["Winning debates", "Wonders", "Special clothing"], "Good character is at the heart of it.", "Ihya' 'Ulum al-Din, Book of Companionship"),
  ]),
 "tz_path": ("Seeking a path", "Taking the first steps towards a guide, without rushing.", [
   ("Start where you are", "Keep the obligations, leave the sins, and hold to a small daily wird: the morning and evening adhkar, some Qur'an, salawat on the Prophet ﷺ and seeking forgiveness. The deeds most loved by Allah are the most constant, even if small.", "Sahih al-Bukhari, Book of Softening the Heart"),
   ("Ask Allah to guide you", "'Those who strive for Us, We will surely guide them to Our paths.' Make du'a sincerely to be shown a guide, and pray istikharah.", "Qur'an, al-'Ankabut 29:69"),
   ("Seek out the company", "Attend gatherings of knowledge and dhikr, sit with the righteous, and ask scholars you trust about the guides they know. A person follows the religion of his close friend.", "Sunan Abi Dawud, Book of Good Manners"),
   ("Do not rush", "Watch before you pledge: is the guide firm on the Shari'ah, knowledgeable, of good character, and does his company bring you closer to Allah? Then take the step, with sincerity and respect, and stay with it.", f"{RQ}, counsel to disciples"),
   ("The seed", "The search itself is a gift from Allah. Whoever turns to Him a hand's span, He turns to him an arm's length, and whoever walks to Him, He comes to him running.", "Sahih al-Bukhari, Book of Tawhid"),
  ], [
   ("Which deeds are most loved by Allah?", "The most constant, even if small", ["The longest", "The most public", "Those done once a year"], "A small wird kept daily beats a large one dropped.", "Sahih al-Bukhari, Book of Softening the Heart"),
   ("Which verse promises guidance to those who strive for Allah?", "Al-'Ankabut 69", ["Al-Baqarah 286", "Al-Ikhlas 4", "Al-Kawthar 1"], "'We will surely guide them to Our paths.'", "Qur'an, al-'Ankabut 29:69"),
   ("Before taking a pledge with a guide, you should…", "Take your time and see that he is firm on the Shari'ah", ["Pledge at the first meeting", "Choose whoever is most famous", "Pick the one who asks least of you"], "Do not rush; watch first.", f"{RQ}, counsel to disciples"),
  ]),
}
